# Submission workspaces. These were ignored wholesale while this repository was
# PUBLIC. It is PRIVATE as of 2026-08-05 and stays private until GitHub confirm the
# server-side GC for the survey-data purge, so the source of two paper submissions is
# now tracked rather than living unversioned on one disk. If the repository is ever
# made public again, revisit this block BEFORE flipping visibility.
#
# Tracked:   manuscript source, evidence scripts, figure generators, configs, the
#            shipped figures, and the analysis record.
# Not tracked: run outputs, caches, and smoke-test artefacts, below.
#
# `runs/` holds two different kinds of thing and they must not share a rule.
# Run OUTPUTS (audit CSVs, manifests, smoke artefacts) are data -- not tracked.
# But the isolated config a run READS, its launch document, and its probe scripts
# are the provenance record: the whole point of an isolated config root is that
# the manifest can name the exact config bytes the run consumed. With the config
# untracked, the manifest records a commit that does not describe the config --
# which is the defect the first-attempt experiment exists to stop repeating,
# reproduced on the experiment meant to fix it.
# The block comment above already says "Tracked: ... configs ..."; until
# 2026-08-05 the rule below contradicted it.
SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/**
!SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/config_*/
!SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/config_*/**
# Records at ANY depth, not only as direct children of runs/. The first version of these
# lines matched `runs/*.md` only, so a provenance attestation written into `runs/_logs/`
# was silently untracked -- a record about a run, excluded by the rule meant to keep
# records about runs. `.sh` is here because launch scripts are launch documents; the
# global `*.sh` rule at the top of this file is for machine-specific automation, and a
# launch script that pins an experiment's parameters is the opposite of that.
!SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/**/
!SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/**/*.md
!SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/**/*.py
!SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/**/*.sh
!SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/**/*.txt
# ...but never a run's own output, whatever directory it lands in.
SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/*/simulation_log.csv
SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/*/*_governance_audit.csv
SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/*/reflection_log.jsonl
SUBMISSION_2026/**/runs/*/raw/**
SUBMISSION_2026/**/__pycache__/
SUBMISSION_2026/**/*.pyc
ICLR2027/experiments/_smoke_*/
ICLR2027/**/__pycache__/

# Rejected image-generation candidates: three ~3.5 MB rasters kept on disk as the
# provenance record for why each was refused. The README beside them carries the
# reasoning, which is the part worth versioning.
SUBMISSION_2026/TAE/_archive/**/candidates/*.pdf

# LaTeX build output. The .tex and .bib ARE tracked -- they are the source. These
# are regenerated by every pdflatex run, and the PDF alone is 1.5 MB per build.
# When a paper is actually submitted, track that exact PDF deliberately with
# `git add -f <path>` so the submitted artefact is in history; a build-directory
# PDF that changes on every compile is not that.
# (These surfaced on 2026-08-05 when the bare `paper/` rule was anchored to the
# repo root -- until then they were hidden by a rule that was also hiding the
# conference manuscript.)
*.aux
*.bbl
*.blg
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz
*.toc
ICLR2027/submission/paper/*.log
ICLR2027/submission/paper/*.out
ICLR2027/submission/paper/*.pdf
SUBMISSION_2026/**/paper/*.log
SUBMISSION_2026/**/paper/*.out
SUBMISSION_2026/**/paper/*.pdf
